GENERAL

VW Parameter Files are simple XML files for storing parameters for VW
compute object (such as Plessey or RANSAC).  The expected file
structure is as follows:

<param_list_type>
  <parameter1>value or could be a string</parameter1>
  <parameter2>value or could be a string</parameter2>
  <parameter3>value or could be a string</parameter3>
  ....
</param_list_type>
<param_list_type2>
  ....
</param_list_type2>
....

eg.

<PlesseyParams>
  <StrengthThreshold> 0.25 </StrengthThreshold>
  <GaussianSigma> 1.0 </GaussianSigma>
  <PatchSize> 5 </PatchSize>
  <EdgePhobia> 0.1 </EdgePhobia>
</PlesseyParams>
<CornerMatcherParams>
  ...
</CornerMatcherParams>

Read the PARAMPARSER section for restrictions placed on file format by
the parser.


PARAMPARSER

ParameterParser is a simple parser for extracting the data from a
parameter file.  It is not very robust so if the file is ill-formed it
will simply fail to pick up the correct values, or any values.  I
expect it may be modified to use standard XML parsing libraries, but
at the moment it's nice that we're not relying on their existence.

Everything between <field> and </field> is taken as its contents,
including white space.  If you request a number from the data field it
will be extracted as a C++ string and then converted, so if there is
no number in there, it will return whatever stringstream returns in
that case (usually 0).  This also means that you can pad out values
with spaces for formatting, if you like, because they will be ignored
when the string is converted.  Everything outside label blocks is
ignored, as is everything before the parameter list's <name> block.

Using ParameterParser: The ParameterParser constructor takes a string
parameter, which is the name expected in the <param_list_type> field, 
and a filename string which defaults to
".params".  It will then parse the file, and extract an associated
map<string, string> of parameter names as keys and string data as the
values from the first <parameter list> block it finds with that name.
If the file is ill-formed, meaning no </parameter list> is found
before the end of the file, then operator bool () will return false.
The results can then be examined using the access function const
map<string, string>& Parameters(), or by requesting the data field for
a particular parameter using the GetValue() functions, which return
false if it isn't there.

A typical use of ParameterParser might go something like this:

class ComputeObject {
  ...
  // Parameters
  unsigned int sizex;
  unsigned int sizey;
  double thresh;
  int num_cycles;
  ...
  void GetParamsFromFile (string filename = ".params");
  ...
}

void ComputeObject::GetParamsFromFile (string filename = ".params")
{
  ParameterParser parser ("ComputeObject", filename);
  if (!parser) cout << "Can't read parameter file " << filename << endl;

  // Modify each parameter in turn if it is in the file, otherwise
  // leave it unchanged
  parser.GetValue ("sizex", sizex);
  parser.GetValue ("sizey", sizey);
  parser.GetValue ("threshold", thresh);
  parser.GetValue ("number of cycles", num_cycles);
}

The GetParamsFromFile method would probably be placed in the header
file so that everything that needs to be changed if the parameters
change is in the same place.

You can also use Extract() to extract from the parsed file the data
associated with each parameter name in an array passed to the object.
This may be preferred from the point of view of neatness, but it
probably won't require less effort to maintain.  Below is an example
equivalent to the above using Extract().  I personally wouldn't use
#defines but it's one way of doing it:

#define NAME_OF_OBJECT "ComputeObject"
#define NUM_PARAMS 4
#define PARAMETER_NAMES {"sizex", "sizey", "threshold", "number of cycles"}
#define DEFAULT_VALUES {3.0, 3.0, 0.2, 100.0}

void ComputeObject::GetParamsFromFile (string filename = ".params")
{
  string parameter_names[NUM_PARAMS] = PARAMETER_NAMES;
  double values[NUM_PARAMS] = DEFAULT_VALUES;

  if (ParameterParser (NAME_OF_OBJECT, parameter_names, values,
		   NUM_PARAMS, filename)) {
	sizex = floor (values[0]);
	sizey = floor (values[1]);
	threshold = values[2];
	num_cycles = int (values[3]);
  }
  else cout << "Can't read file " << filename << endl;
}